#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#     "capabilities-contract>=0.22,<1",
#     "httpx>=0.27",
#     "qrcode>=7.4",
#     "telethon==1.45.0",
# ]
# ///
"""
telegram — agent-first CLI for a personal Telegram account (MTProto, full account).

This tool is designed to be invoked by agents. Run `telegram help` to load the full
usage contract on demand — do not rely on memorised knowledge of its surface.

Agent startup protocol (this is a STATEFUL CLI — it holds a login session):
  1. Run `telegram doctor` first. It proves the chain: credentials resolve, Telegram
     is reachable, and the session is authorized.
  2. Exit 2 from doctor means the session is missing or expired — run
     `telegram login` (shows a QR to scan from an already authorized Telegram
     app, then prompts for the 2FA password if set), or `telegram login --phone`
     (prompts for phone, the code Telegram sends, and a 2FA password if set).
     Login persists the session file; later commands reuse it with no prompt.
  3. A healthy `telegram doctor` (exit 0) means ready to work.

The app every account signs in through is registered once at my.telegram.org/apps
and given to the machine once: `telegram setup set --field TELEGRAM_API_ID=<id>
--stdin` with {"TELEGRAM_API_HASH": "<hash>"} on stdin. A connection naming no
`api_id` or `secret_env` of its own inherits it, and `telegram app-check` asks
Telegram whether it accepts it before any account is linked.

Configuration requires explicit connections with a positive
`expected_account_id`, including the one-account case. The selected connection's app credentials and session resolve by the
cascade (first non-empty wins): the `--session` flag
(session path only) > a project .env(.local) > ~/.config/telegram/credentials.env
> the process environment. Secrets (TELEGRAM_API_HASH, DEEPGRAM_API_KEY) have no
flag — only their env-var NAME resolves them, never the command line.

More than one account is declared in a connections registry — the project's
own, else the machine-level ~/.config/telegram/connections.json (the natural
home for a personal account); where this project keeps its own, and in what
order they are read, "Where connections live" below says. An entry may name an app of its own - its id literally and
its hash by env-key indirection - and otherwise signs in through the machine's;
each owns a per-connection session:

  {
    "default": "marvin",
    "connections": {
      "marvin": { "name": "Marvin AI", "username": "marvin_kz",
                       "expected_account_id": 8200881535,
                       "api_id": 1234567, "secret_env": "TELEGRAM_API_HASH",
                       "allow_write": true }
    }
  }

Every live connection declares the positive Telegram user/account ID in
`expected_account_id`; it is distinct from the API application `api_id` and
need not equal the registry label. Discovery login may run before this binding;
doctor, service and live takeover refuse an absent or mismatched identity.

The write gate: outbound sends and reactions leave the system, so they are off
unless a connection grants them. Grant them with
`"allow_write": true` on a connection's grant. A refused write exits
4 — ask the user, never lift the gate yourself.

Outside any project, connections, doctor and app-check are machine reads:
they use the machine's own connections read-only, unless a grant switched one
off. login uses them there too, so a connection declared globally is linked on
the machine; every other verb that needs a connection is refused there.

  --connection <id>        Select a connection; accepted by every verb. Optional
                           with a default pointer or a sole connection.
  TELEGRAM_API_ID          Numeric app id from my.telegram.org/apps: the machine's
                           app, which every connection naming no `api_id` inherits.
  TELEGRAM_API_HASH        App hash paired with the id (secret); a connection naming
                           no `secret_env` inherits it.
  TELEGRAM_SESSION         Session path, no extension — Telethon writes
                           <path>.session on disk. Optional one-shot override; the
                           default connection's session defaults to
                           $XDG_STATE_HOME/telegram/session, a named connection's
                           to <state-dir>/<id>/session.
  DEEPGRAM_API_KEY         Required only by `transcribe` (secret). Shared: kept once
                           for the machine in ~/.config/capabilities/shared.env,
                           which `telegram setup set` writes, asked before
                           telegram's own credentials.env.

  --session <PATH>         One-shot override of the session-file path, accepted
                           anywhere on the line. Names a path, not a secret.

Commands:
  telegram help                                Print this document.
  telegram doctor                              Health check: creds + reachability + session.
  telegram login [--phone [NUMBER]] [--events] Interactive first-time auth: by default scan
                                               a QR from an authorized app (`--qr` names it
                                               explicitly); `--phone` signs in with the
                                               phone number and the code Telegram sends.
                                               `--events` drives it with no terminal: JSON
                                               lines on stdout (qr, with a new one each
                                               time one expires, for up to five minutes;
                                               phone_needed; code_needed; password_needed
                                               with the account's hint; done; error) and
                                               each answer as one line on stdin. A first
                                               login under `--events` that fails or is
                                               cancelled (SIGTERM, SIGINT, SIGHUP) removes
                                               the connection and the session file it
                                               made. The session file is owner-only (0600).
  telegram app-check                           Ask Telegram whether it accepts the app
                                               this machine signs in through (`--connection`
                                               names a connection's app instead), by asking
                                               for a login token: no account is touched and
                                               no code is sent. `status` is `accepted`
                                               (exit 0), `invalid` (Telegram does not know
                                               the id and hash; exit 2), or `refused`, with
                                               `reason` `blocked` (exit 2), `flood_limited`
                                               and `retry_after` seconds (exit 5), or
                                               `other` (exit 5); `message` says why in
                                               a sentence a person can be shown, and
                                               `error` carries what Telegram answered.
  telegram whoami                              Identity of the signed-in account.
  telegram chats [--limit N]                   List recent dialogs. Beside `id`, `name`,
                                               `unread` and `kind` (the raw Telegram
                                               class), each entry settles what the chat
                                               is: `category` is `user`, `bot`, `group`
                                               (a legacy group, a supergroup or a
                                               gigagroup), `channel` (a broadcast only
                                               its admins post in), or `unknown` when the
                                               class settles nothing; and `forum` is true
                                               when the chat's messages live in topics,
                                               which `telegram topics` lists.
  telegram topics <chat> [--limit N] [--query TEXT]
                                               List a forum group's topics; each
                                               `id` is the root message id the
                                               send verbs take as --topic.
  telegram read <chat> [--limit N] [--since ISO] [--topic TOPIC_ID] [--json]
                                               Read messages from a chat. A read
                                               of a forum spans every one of its
                                               topics unless --topic names one
                                               by its root message id, the id
                                               `telegram topics` prints and the
                                               send verbs take; a named topic
                                               carries both the messages posted
                                               straight into it and the replies
                                               threaded inside it, and --limit
                                               counts what comes back. Each JSON
                                               record carries the `topic_id` it
                                               came from: a root message id, 1
                                               for a forum's General topic, and
                                               null in a chat whose messages
                                               live in no topic at all.
  telegram search <chat> <query> [--limit N] [--topic TOPIC_ID] [--json]
                                               Search messages inside a chat;
                                               the same forum-topic scoping and
                                               `topic_id` as `read`.
  telegram send <chat> <text> [--reply-to MSG_ID | --topic TOPIC_ID] [--external]
            [--no-link-preview]
                                               Send a message. In a forum, a
                                               message lands in the topic named
                                               by --topic (its root message id)
                                               or in the topic owning the
                                               --reply-to message; with neither
                                               it lands in General.
                                               --no-link-preview sends it
                                               without the preview card Telegram
                                               attaches to a link; the service's
                                               own messages take the same switch
                                               as `defaults.link_preview`.
  telegram send-media <chat> <path> [--caption TEXT]
            [--reply-to MSG_ID | --topic TOPIC_ID]
                                                 Send a local media file; the
                                                 same forum-topic addressing.
  telegram react <chat> <msgid> <emoji> [...]  Add one or more emoji reactions.
  telegram download <chat> <msgid> [--json]    Materialize one message's media to a
                                               deterministic per-connection cache
                                               path (download only if absent);
                                               prints the absolute path.
  telegram export <chat> --out PATH [--media-dir PATH] [--limit N] [--since ISO]
            [--topic TOPIC_ID] [--no-photos] [--no-stickers] [--videos] [--documents]
                                               Dump full history to JSON; download
                                               voice/audio (+photos/stickers by
                                               default) to media-dir; leave
                                               transcription placeholders. The
                                               same forum-topic scoping and
                                               `topic_id` as `read`.
  telegram transcribe <json_path> [--force] [--model nova-3]
                                               Fill transcription placeholders in an
                                               exported JSON via Deepgram (audio
                                               referenced by media.file_path).
  telegram service init [--connection ID] [--force]
                                               Create project-local service settings
                                               and context templates.
  telegram service migrate-ownership --connection ID
            --confirm-all-legacy-daemons-stopped
                                               One-time fail-closed cutover to
                                               account-global daemon ownership.
  telegram service migrate-profiles [--apply]
                                               Move the old worker settings to
                                               profile names: each distinct
                                               worker and its model, effort,
                                               speed and context becomes one
                                               project profile named from the
                                               values, and settings, the
                                               channel register and open job
                                               rows name it. A dry run unless
                                               --apply; --apply needs the
                                               service stopped. Prints the
                                               TELEGRAM_SERVICE_PROFILE value
                                               that replaces a set TG_WORKER or
                                               TELEGRAM_SERVICE_WORKER.
  telegram service schema                      Print the settings the service
                                               holds itself to as JSON Schema
                                               (draft 2020-12), with what a
                                               panel needs under x- keys:
                                               x-apply (reload or restart),
                                               x-keyed-by and x-entry-label of
                                               each map, x-inherits (where an
                                               absent value comes from),
                                               x-layers, x-document (a prompt
                                               file and its document key) and
                                               x-documents (the service's own
                                               prompts).
  telegram service validate [--scope project|global] [--key KEY]
                                               Judge proposed settings exactly
                                               as `service reload` would and
                                               write nothing: stdin carries the
                                               scope's whole document, or with
                                               --key the JSON value of that key
                                               (dotted for a nested one) put
                                               over the document held now.
                                               Refused with exit 6
                                               service_settings_invalid naming
                                               the full path the reload names.
                                               Accepted, it prints the top-level
                                               keys that change, `apply`
                                               (reload, or restart when
                                               connection or environment
                                               changes) and each prompt file the
                                               settings name with its document
                                               key: `telegram context show|edit|
                                               put <key>` reads and writes it.
  telegram service harness [--json]            The harness tools (claude, codex)
                                               of every profile a turn is
                                               configured to run on now - each
                                               name the settings reach, every
                                               chat's `/set profile` and the
                                               profile of every open job - each
                                               resolved and fitted as a turn
                                               resolves it, for the sign-ins a
                                               node needs. Runs no turn and
                                               writes nothing; `unresolved`
                                               names a profile that does not
                                               resolve or fit.
  telegram service doctor|run|start|reload|stop|status|logs
                                               Manage the bundled Telegram assistant
                                               service for this project. The engine
                                               runs from the installed capability
                                               bundle; the project stores only
                                               capabilities/telegram/service config.
                                               Status follows the account-global
                                               owner record and reports actual code,
                                               project and state provenance.
                                               Every worker turn - dialogue,
                                               job, voice task, call summary -
                                               runs through callva-harness-
                                               runner on the profile its room
                                               names: the channel's /set
                                               profile, a forum topic's,
                                               group's or direct user's
                                               `profile`, then
                                               `defaults.profile` (over it,
                                               TELEGRAM_SERVICE_PROFILE), else
                                               `stub`; a call's task and the
                                               call summary run on
                                               `defaults.voice_agent.profile`,
                                               then the default. `start` and
                                               `run` refuse with exit 6
                                               profile_refused when a name the
                                               settings reach does not resolve
                                               or fit, and with exit 6
                                               profiles_migration_required while
                                               settings, the channel register
                                               or the environment still name a
                                               worker (`worker`, `workers`,
                                               TELEGRAM_SERVICE_WORKER,
                                               TG_WORKER). `doctor` reports
                                               each distinct profile the
                                               settings name as `resolved` with
                                               its harness, model, layer and
                                               version, or `failed` when it does
                                               not resolve or fit, and starts
                                               no session. `doctor --live` also
                                               spends one live turn per
                                               profile, held to
                                               `defaults.worker_timeout`, and
                                               reports the harness's own
                                               verdict in place of `resolved`:
                                               a model the harness refuses, a
                                               harness missing from PATH or
                                               older than the runner supports,
                                               one that cannot authenticate, or
                                               one that gives no verdict in
                                               time is not ok.
                                               `doctor` exits 5 when its own
                                               verdict is `ok: false`, so a
                                               script can gate on it.
                                               The job register lives in the
                                               project's Postgres database, in
                                               telegram_* tables migrated on
                                               first use; `doctor` reports
                                               whether it opens and which level
                                               named it. The database is the
                                               first that answers: the
                                               project's .env.local / .env,
                                               then the environment, then the
                                               machine's store setting
                                               (`capabilities store show` shows
                                               which). With none, `start`, `run`
                                               and `jobs` exit 6
                                               store_not_configured.
  telegram prompts list | show <key> [--version ID] | versions <key>
            | put <key> <file|-> [--base ID] [--note TEXT]
            | import [DIR] [--note TEXT] | export [DIR]
                                               The service's prompts - context,
                                               delegation, job-worker, the voice
                                               prompts and each context/<x> - as
                                               versions in telegram_prompts in the
                                               project's store. The service reads
                                               a key's active version, and its
                                               file under capabilities/telegram/
                                               service only while the store holds
                                               no version of that key. `import`
                                               saves each file there (or in DIR)
                                               whose text is not the active
                                               version; `export` writes each
                                               active version back as its file,
                                               leaving an equal one alone. `put`
                                               saves a new version and keeps the
                                               earlier ones readable through
                                               `versions` and `show --version`;
                                               `--base` names the version
                                               replaced, and a save that lost the
                                               race exits 6 conflict. A worker
                                               saves only for a requester whose
                                               role may /set.
  telegram profiles list | show <name> | save <name> <file|-> [--base ID]
                                               The harness profiles a worker runs
                                               on, kept in the project's store.
                                               Every name starts with telegram-;
                                               one resolves to this project's
                                               version, then the global one, then
                                               the bundled telegram-claude and
                                               telegram-codex. `list` shows each
                                               with its layer, version, harness,
                                               model, effort, rights, timeout and
                                               fit; `show` adds its text. `save`
                                               writes a new version at this
                                               project's scope only, refusing a
                                               bundled or global name, a profile
                                               the runner or fit refuses, and a
                                               `${`; `--base` names the version
                                               replaced, and a save that lost the
                                               race exits 6 conflict. A worker
                                               saves only for a requester whose
                                               role may /set. The runner is
                                               pinned in the bundle's
                                               service/profiles.py, which these
                                               verbs run.

Chat references:
  <chat> can be @username, a phone (+1555…), a numeric id, or a chat title
  (case-insensitive substring matched against your dialog list).

I/O contract:
  - Success: JSON on stdout, exit 0. (`read`/`search` without --json emit a
    human-readable transcript as a single text block.)
  - Progress for long runs (export, transcribe) goes to STDERR; stdout stays
    clean for the final result.
  - Error:   {"error": {"code", "message", "hint"?, "status"?}} on stderr,
             non-zero exit.

Contract commands (the same on every capability):
  telegram stub                                 One-line awareness text.
  telegram manifest --json                      Machine-readable declaration;
                                                 `connections.device_bound` is
                                                 true: a connection is a session
                                                 minted on one device.
  telegram connections                          Where every connection's values
                                                 resolve from — tier, source,
                                                 secrets masked (local, no network).
                                                 Each connection carries `session`
                                                 {live, account}: `live` is true
                                                 when its session file on this
                                                 machine holds an auth key and the
                                                 signed-in account Telethon keeps
                                                 beside it, and no service here
                                                 recorded it unauthorized since it
                                                 last started; `account` is the
                                                 account the connection records
                                                 (the one login/doctor verified,
                                                 else expected_account_id), or null.
  telegram guide [<topic>]                      Guide topics / one guide body,
                                                 fetched live from the docs base.
  telegram ids list|get <l>|set <l> <v>|rm <l>  The project identifiers envelope
                [--note <text>]                  (capabilities/telegram/identifiers.json,
                                                  or store rows once the agent moved).
  telegram refs                                 Menu of the project's reference
                                                 files for this capability.
  telegram inventory [--network]                What this capability holds in
                                                this project (--network adds
                                                remote detail).
  telegram migrate [status]                     Apply the job register's pending
                                                steps to the machine's store,
                                                after the harness runner's
                                                profile table under its ledger
                                                `harness`; `status` reports
                                                where both stand (the runner's
                                                under `harness`), without a
                                                lock, changing nothing.

Exit codes:
  0  success
  2  auth — missing/invalid credentials, session expired, not logged in
  3  not found — the chat or entity could not be resolved
  4  policy refusal — disabled by effective project/global policy records,
     or `send` on a read-only connection — ask the user, never lift the gate
     yourself
  5  server / network — Telegram unreachable, flood-wait, Deepgram 5xx, and
     `service doctor` reporting `ok: false` — a running daemon whose update
     sync is not fresh, a profile the settings name that does not resolve
     or fit or, under `--live`, that its harness did not accept, or a job
     register the store did not open; after its report it prints the error
     envelope naming the first of these (`service_unhealthy` with the
     daemon's last error, `worker_unavailable`, or the store's own code),
     which a supervisor records as why the service cannot work. A route
     skipped on this node is named under `skipped_settings` beside
     `warnings`, and the doctor stays ok
  6  input / validation — a malformed argument, a bad date, a machine with
     no store configured for the service or its jobs, a profile the
     service settings name that does not resolve or fit, or a worker still
     named where a profile is (run `telegram service migrate-profiles`)

Quirks worth knowing:
  - This is a full-account MTProto session — a personal user account, so it
    sees everything that account sees. The session file is auth material — it
    grants account access; keep it out of the repo.
  - A flood-wait (too many requests) surfaces as exit 5 with the wait seconds
    in the message; back off and retry later.
"""

